    "nickname": "kozuechan",
    "content": "Since there is a trade-off, I would say it depends on why you want to increase n.\n\nIf increasing n prevents an inefficient solver from passing the test cases, that is a strong justification for doing so. Given that the number of search nodes in this kind of backtracking problem typically grows exponentially with n, this seems plausible. Of course, it is hard to say for sure without implementing a solver and running experiments. If a naive solver can already handle n = 26, there is hardly any point in increasing n.",
    "codingamerHandle": "00ff9c85fcc84fd753d84c98ec18ca0a6986723",
